         "id": "SECTION_images",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Images from Mexico City Museo\n\nThese images are copyright for Mexico City Museo and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgMexicoCityMuseo0\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMexicoCityMuseo0', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Aztec Sun Stone on display at the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico City (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).<br />Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the Museum.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Mesoamerican calendars - They elevated multiplication to an extraordinary art form. (<htcite2>)</li><li>Understanding the Multiplication Table (6 and 7) - In this text, we will explore how the Mesopotamian people related to these tables. (<htcite3>)</li><li>Cradles of civilization (Part I) - While humanity originated in Africa, the first civilizations emerged in various regions around the world, such as Mesopotamia, the Andes, and Mesoamerica. (<htcite4>)</li><li>Together we are strong - In this text, we see how the four operations, when combined, open the doors to so much knowledge (<htcite5>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">2 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgMexicoCityMuseo1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMexicoCityMuseo1', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Poster of the human skeleton describing <i>Homo sapiens</i>. At the Museo Nacional de Antropología in Mexico (<htcite6>). Photograph taken on <htdate1>, during a visit to the Museum.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Humans - Who are we? Why are we alone today, when in the past we lived with others? (<htcite7>)</li><li>Exercising with numbers - We do a physical exercise with the numbers to reinforce the addition of units and tens. (<htcite8>)</li><li>Multiplying with the body (Level 1) - In this text, we deepen our knowledge about the evolution of human beings, in addition to multiplying using our own bodies. (<htcite9>)</li><li>Walking and singing - Who was the first person in the family to walk? What is the relationship of this to indigenous numbers? (<htcite10>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">3 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgMexicoCityMuseo2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMexicoCityMuseo2', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Mammoth fossil exhibited in Mexico. Photograph taken on <htdate2>, during a visit to the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico (<htcite11>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>The arrival in Abya Yala (America) - In this text, we will explore different studies on the period when the first peoples could have arrived in <i>Abya Yala</i> (America) (<htcite12>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">4 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgMexicoCityMuseo3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMexicoCityMuseo3', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Writing present at the National Museum of Anthropology - Mexico (<htcite13>). Photo taken on <htdate3>, during a visit to the museum.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Building to count (Suanpan, 算盤) - In this text, we see the differences and what is in common between the Chinese abacus and the Japanese abacus. (<htcite14>)</li></ul></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
